  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 231 AM EDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\nHot for October to continue today thanks to the highly anomalous \nridge to our south. Moisture riding up and over the ridge will spark \nsome showers and storms north and northwest of East Tennessee and \nsouthwest VA. It is conceivable, as shown in a few HRRR runs, that \nlight rain from dying cells reaches the northern Plateau like Scott \nCounty TN late this evening into tonight, but rainfall will remain \ninconsequential. Otherwise we will be again near daily record highs \nboth today and tomorrow.\n\nDate    Chattanooga    Knoxville      Tri-Cities     Oak Ridge                 \n10-14   87(1954)       87(1881)       85(1954)       85(1954)       \n10-15   85(1950)       87(1897)       83(1971)       83(2021)     \n\nThere is again very low potential for a rain shower Thursday \nafternoon, but is likely confined to southwestern Virginia per \nensemble consensus with guidance depicting very meager CAPE profiles.\n\nA phased upper trough will bring about a polar front into the \nEastern US for Friday. Despite rain chances being quite high for \nportions of northeastern TN into VA, overall rain amounts are \npitiful thanks to the shallow vertical moisture profiles. Light rain \nshowers will pass through both along the front, and post-frontal \ninto Friday evening. Winds may be a bit breezy Friday afternoon, \nchilly as cold air advection starts working behind the front in a \nnorthwest flow. Saturday, then, is much cooler versus Friday and \nsets us up for a cool couple of nights. For the Tri-Cities, this \nfrontal passage is right on time to experience the first bout of \ntemperatures favorable for frost. Those with temperature sensitive \nplants should consider bringing them in definitely for Saturday \nnight, as well as Sunday night, especially in northern locales. Best \nchances for temperatures less than 37F remain in elevated terrain \nand north of the Knoxville Metropolitan area. Temperatures then will \nslightly rebound going into early next week as we maintain a below \naverage precipitation stance for the foreseeable future.",
